    {
      "id": "n4vv1b7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4.3 Industries"
    },
    {
      "id": "t387xie",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Automobile Industry"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Chennai-Hosur corridor is a major automotive hub. Chennai accounts for nearly 35% of India's automobile production and 40% of auto component exports. Major companies: Hyundai (Sriperumbudur), Ford (Maraimalai Nagar, now closed), BMW assembly (Chennai), Daimler (Oragadam), Renault-Nissan (Oragadam), Royal Enfield (Oragadam and Tiruvottiyur), TVS Motor Company (Hosur).",
      "id": "o6btj9c"
    },
    {
      "id": "at7xd7h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Textile Industry"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tamil Nadu is India's largest textile producer. Coimbatore ('Manchester of South India'), Tiruppur ('Knit Capital of India'), and Erode are major textile hubs. Kancheepuram (Kanjivaram silk), Bhavani (Bhavani Jamakkalam carpet), and Madurai (cotton dhotis) are known for traditional textiles.",
      "id": "vms977q"
    },
    {
      "id": "22q85rc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "IT and Electronics"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Chennai is the second largest IT hub in India after Bengaluru. Major IT companies including TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, Accenture, and Capgemini have large operations in Chennai. TIDEL Park in Taramani was one of India's first IT parks.",
      "id": "o5bkyso"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10ka7i",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Leather Industry"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tamil Nadu produces about 60% of India's leather. Vellore, Ranipet, and Chennai are the major centres. Ambur (Vellore district) is famous for leather footwear.",
      "id": "tp7tjzc"
    },
    {
      "id": "tvnd5n5",
      "type": "heading",
